    "description": "Airport management brings together the operation, maintenance, documentation, and safety coordination of an aerodrome. Iran Airports and Air Navigation Company is documented as a major airport operator, but operator descriptions alone do not establish the safety performance or current certification of individual airports.",
    "tags": ["Airports", "Airport management", "Aerodrome certification", "Safety management", "Iran"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T13:02:38Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T13:02:38Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Airport Management in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran’s airport operating structure and the distinction between airport management, certification, and demonstrated safety performance."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "operating-network",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An operating network with several responsibilities",
        "content": "An airport is an operating organization as well as a physical site. The Airports Council International Asia-Pacific and Middle East directory identifies Iran Airports and Air Navigation Company as an operator of airports across Iran. Its entry includes Mehrabad in Tehran, Mashhad, Shiraz, Tabriz, Bandar Abbas, and numerous smaller locations. The accompanying organizational description places airport leadership, management, maintenance, development, air navigation, and air traffic management among the company’s duties and describes its affiliation with the Ministry of Roads and Urban Development. [s1]\n\nThat description explains institutional scope, rather than demonstrated results. It does not establish that every airport in Iran has the same operator or that every listed facility offers the same services. The directory does not provide a dated airport-by-airport certification register, audited operating results, or a consistent annual performance series. Its network totals therefore should not be treated as verified October 2026 statistics. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "certification-and-operations",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Management and certification have different roles",
        "content": "ICAO’s aerodrome certification programme distinguishes the work of civil aviation authorities from the work of airport operators. Authorities develop certification rules and undertake certification and oversight; operators need the technical knowledge and procedures to meet the applicable standards. The programme identifies operational procedures, a safety management system, an aerodrome manual, and monitoring processes as central elements of certification. An aerodrome is the operational site used for aircraft arrivals, departures, and movement. [s2]\n\nFor readers assessing an Iranian airport, these distinctions make the evidence easier to interpret. A directory entry establishes an operator’s identity. A certificate and its conditions address regulatory authorization. A manual describes operating arrangements, while monitoring addresses continuing compliance. None of these documents alone supplies a complete account of service quality or actual safety outcomes. This is an editorial framework drawn from ICAO’s separation of certification, documentation, and monitoring functions, rather than a finding about any particular Iranian airport. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "people-and-safety",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The organization behind the infrastructure",
        "content": "Material presented by Airports Council International at an ICAO certification workshop emphasizes the operator’s organizational capability. Its discussion of on-site verification asks whether management has committed to safety, whether staff responsibilities and reporting lines are defined, and whether the operator monitors training for employees and subcontractors. These are international assessment principles; they are not evidence that a particular Iranian operator has passed an assessment. [s3]\n\nThe distinction matters when considering airport development. Buildings and flight surfaces are visible, but an assessment also needs to examine the people and procedures supporting their operation. The workshop material treats management competence, responsibility, and training as subjects for verification. A useful account of airport management must therefore connect physical facilities with documented organizational arrangements, rather than infer effective operation from infrastructure alone. [s3]\n\nIran’s airport network can be introduced through the operator directory and these international concepts. A publication-ready assessment of its current management still needs dated Iranian certification records, local operating documentation, and evidence of results. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "certification-framework",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "An international framework for understanding airport certification",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Civil aviation authority\"] -->|\"Develops certification rules and conducts oversight\"| B[\"Aerodrome certification process\"]\n  C[\"Airport operator\"] -->|\"Develops operating documentation\"| D[\"Aerodrome manual\"]\n  C -->|\"Implements operational safety arrangements\"| E[\"Safety management system\"]\n  D -->|\"Documentation considered in certification\"| B\n  E -->|\"System considered in certification\"| B\n  B -->|\"Includes monitoring for regulatory compliance\"| F[\"Continuing compliance monitoring\"]\n```",
        "summary": "This diagram summarizes ICAO’s general certification framework. It does not depict verified reporting lines in Iran or certify any Iranian airport.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
